WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.898
Evidence and documentation are what turn response activity into an accountable investigation.

2
00:00:05.018 --> 00:00:15.630
During an incident, people will ask what happened, when it happened, what systems were affected, what actions were taken, who approved them, and what remains unresolved.

3
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:25.954
If responders do not document as they work, the team may lose the timeline, repeat steps, miss decisions, or struggle to explain the incident later.

4
00:00:26.074 --> 00:00:39.544
A playbook should tell responders what to record. At minimum, the case record should include timestamps, systems, accounts, users, alerts, evidence sources, responder actions, decisions, approvals, containment steps, recovery steps, and open questions.

5
00:00:39.664 --> 00:00:49.460
The record should distinguish between observed facts and current interpretation. "Observed a successful login from an unusual location" is different from "confirmed account compromise.

6
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:58.151
" "Suspicious process detected" is different from "malware confirmed. " Careful language protects the integrity of the response and reduces confusion with stakeholders.

7
00:00:58.271 --> 00:01:06.026
Evidence types vary by scenario. Endpoint telemetry may show processes, files, command lines, network connections, detections, and isolation status.

8
00:01:06.146 --> 00:01:13.493
Authentication logs may show successful and failed logins, role assumptions, token activity, new devices, or multi-factor prompts.

9
00:01:13.613 --> 00:01:20.960
Cloud audit logs may show resource creation, policy changes, key use, network exposure changes, or service configuration changes.

10
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:27.202
Email headers and message metadata can support phishing analysis. Network logs can show connection patterns.

11
00:01:27.322 --> 00:01:38.343
Screenshots may preserve a user-visible condition. Memory or disk captures may be appropriate for high-value systems when the organization has trained staff and approved procedures.

12
00:01:38.463 --> 00:01:44.177
Evidence handling should be practical and proportional. Not every minor alert requires forensic imaging.

13
00:01:44.297 --> 00:01:58.175
But every incident needs a reliable record. The playbook should identify where evidence is stored, who can access it, how files are named, how sensitive evidence is protected, and how long records are retained.

14
00:01:58.295 --> 00:02:10.948
It should also warn responders not to alter evidence casually. For example, opening a suspicious file on an uncontrolled system or forwarding sensitive data through informal channels can create new risk.

15
00:02:11.068 --> 00:02:20.864
Chain of custody is the documented history of who controlled evidence, when they received it, how it was stored, and when it was transferred.

16
00:02:20.984 --> 00:02:27.922
At a practical level, it helps show that evidence was handled consistently and was not casually modified.

17
00:02:28.042 --> 00:02:34.165
Formal chain of custody may be required for legal, regulatory, employment, or law enforcement matters.

18
00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:39.999
Even when formal custody is not required, disciplined handling improves trust in the record.

19
00:02:40.119 --> 00:02:53.180
Good documentation also supports post-incident improvement. The team can see which detection worked, which evidence was missing, which approval slowed containment, which communication path helped, and which control should be improved.

20
00:02:53.300 --> 00:03:00.239
The playbook should make documentation part of response, not a cleanup chore left until everyone is tired.
